  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...A stalling cold front may bring rain at times\ntoday through Sunday.\n\nCurrent surface analysis depicts an area of low pressure over\nfar eastern Canada with a cold front draped south through New\nEngland and into the Ohio Valley. Mostly light showers are\ncurrently located along this boundary, which will drift\nsoutheast through tonight. Ahead of this, we are well above\naverage in terms of temperatures, with readings in the mid to\nupper 80s for most. Dew points are in the mid 60s, which\ncombined with the warmth should yield at least some minimal CAPE\nvalues this afternoon/evening. As the front drops southward,\nexpect showers to increase in coverage and perhaps pop off a few\nthunderstorms as they move into the slightly unstable air mass.\nThey will also be aided by the right entrance region of an \napproaching jet streak, which should help to overcome the mid-\nlevel drying that is ongoing now in westerly flow. Rainfall \namounts will be generally less than a quarter inch for most \nlocations.\n\nStrong high pressure will pass north of the area Saturday while\nthe front stalls just to our south. Southern stream moisture \nwill likely overrun the boundary fairly far to the north, but \nthere remains some uncertainty in the dividing line between rain\nand no rain. The trend has been for a further north track, so\nhave shifted rain chances about 20 to 30 miles north with the\nlatest forecast package. \n\nA wave of low pressure will ride along this stalled boundary \nSaturday night into Sunday, and this may provide a higher chance\nof some heavier rain spreading northward into the area. The\nWeather Prediction Center does have our eastern areas in a\nMarginal Excessive Rainfall Outlook, and this seems plausible\ngiven the potential for a surge of 2\"+ PWATs. Rain that does \noccur will likely exit the area Sunday evening.\n\nBeing north of the boundary and potentially having a lot of\ncloud cover, high temperatures will likely remain in the 60s for\nmost areas over the weekend.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...First widespread frost and freeze potential west of \nthe Blue Ridge early next week.\n\nA distinct pattern shift unfolds into the first full week of \nOctober. A highly amplified trough characterized by mid-level \nheights around 531-dm is expected to cross through Quebec and New \nBrunswick on Monday. While the core of the forcing remains across \nNew England and points northward, the main impact will come with the \ncold air arriving in the wake of this trough. A robust area of \nCanadian high pressure moves into the area Monday night through mid-\nweek. Consequently, this is forecast to bring a true autumnal feel \nto the air with daily temperatures well below average. For Tuesday \nand Wednesday, forecast highs will generally range from the upper \n50s to 60s, with mountain locales mainly in the 50s. Looking at \novernight conditions, the dry nature of the air mass allows \ntemperatures to plummet at night. Areas west of the Blue Ridge can \nexpect widespread mid/upper 30s on Monday and Tuesday nights. This \nwill also include some pockets of upper 20s to near the freezing \nmark. Given the growing season continues across the region, there \nwill be a threat of some frost/freeze conditions both of those \nnights. For those with sensitive outdoor vegetation, plans should be \nmade to ensure their safety in these conditions.\n\nAlthough Canadian high pressure moves off the coast toward the \nmid/late week period, the overall pattern will remain dry throughout \nnext week. Temperatures slowly rebound through the week as forecast \nhighs return to the low/mid 70s. Nighttime lows remain chilly, but \nthe threat for frost/freeze conditions should diminish beyond \nWednesday morning.",
